<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wusun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2nd century BCE–5th century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wusun were a nomadic people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> known from Chinese sources. Displaced from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> region, they drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> from the Ili valley around 130 BCE and ruled the steppes of Zhetysu and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-shan">Tian Shan</a> for several centuries, absorbing the Saka who remained there. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> courted them as allies against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> and sent Chinese princesses to marry their rulers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wusun.jpg" alt="Wusun" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Derzsi Elekes Andor · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:30._Rider_burial_mound_Tenlik_(III.-II._B.C.)_Kazakhstan.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
